an, T.C. Pearson, and C.P. Larsen. <year>1999</year>. Asialo GM1(&#x0002b;) CD8(&#x0002b;) T cells play a critical role in costimulation blockade-resistant allograft rejection. <source>J. Clin. Invest.</source> <volume>104</volume>:<fpage>1715</fpage>&#x02013;1722.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10606625</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib8"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal">Guo, Z., L. Meng, O. Kim, J. Wang, J. Hart, G. He, M.-L. Alegre, J.R. Thistlethwaite, T.C. Pearson, C.P. Larsen, and K.A. Newell. <year>2001</year>. CD8-T cell-mediated rejection of intestinal allografts is resistant to inhibition of the CD40/CD154 costimulatory pathway. <source>Transplantation.</source> <volume>71</volume>:<fpage>1351</fpage>&#x02013;1354.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11403253</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib9"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal">Makhlouf, L., K. Kishimoto, R.N. Smith, R. Abdi, M. Koulmanda, H.J. Winn, H. Auchincloss Jr., and M.H. Sayegh. <year>2002</year>. The role of autoimmunity in islet allograft destruction: major histocompatibility complex class II matching is necessary for autoimmune destruction of allogeneic islet transplants after T-cell costimulatory blockade. <source>Diabetes.</source> <volume>51</volume>:<fpage>3202</fpage>&#x02013;3210.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12401711</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal">Jones, N.D., A. Van Maurik, M. Hara, B.M. Spriewald, O. Witzke, P.J. Morris, and K.J. Wood. <year>2000</year>. CD40-CD40 ligand-independent activation of CD8&#x0002b; T cells can trigger allograft rejection. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>165</volume>:<fpage>1111</fpage>&#x02013;1118.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10878390</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal">van Stipdonk, M.J., G. Hardenberg, M.S. Bijker, E.E. Lemmens, N.M. Droin, D.R. Green, and S.P. Schoenberger. <year>2003</year>. Dynamic programming of CD8&#x0002b; T lymphocyte responses. <source>Nat. Immunol.</source> <volume>4</volume>:<fpage>361</fpage>&#x02013;365.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12640451</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal">van Stipdonk, M.J., E.E. Lemmens, and S.P. Schoenberger. <year>2001</year>. Naive CTLs require a single brief period of antigenic stimulation for clonal expansion and differentiation. <source>Nat. Immunol.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>423</fpage>&#x02013;429.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11323696</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal">Kaech, S.M., and R. Ahmed. <year>2001</year>. Memory CD8&#x0002b; T cell differentiation: initial antigen encounter triggers a developmental program in naive cells. <source>Nat. Immunol.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>415</fpage>&#x02013;422.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11323695</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal">Masopust, D., S.M. Kaech, E.J. Wherry, and R. Ahmed. <year>2004</year>. The role of programming in memory T-cell development. <source>Curr. Opin. Immunol.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>217</fpage>&#x02013;225.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15023416</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal">Marzo, A.L., K.D. Klonowski, A. Le Bon, P. Borrow, D.F. Tough, and L. Lefrancois. <year>2005</year>. Initial T cell frequency dictates memory CD8&#x0002b; T cell lineage commitment. <source>Nat. Immunol.</source> <volume>6</volume>:<fpage>793</fpage>&#x02013;799.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16025119</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal">Hataye, J., J.J. Moon, A. Khoruts, C. Reilly, and M.K. Jenkins. <year>2006</year>. Naive and memory CD4&#x0002b; T cell survival controlled by clonal abundance. <source>Science.</source> <volume>312</volume>:<fpage>114</fpage>&#x02013;116.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16513943</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal">Powell, T.J., D.M. Brown, J.A. Hollenbaugh, T. Charbonneau, R.A. Kemp, S.L. Swain, and R.W. Dutton. <year>2004</year>. CD8&#x0002b; T cells responding to influenza infection reach and persist at higher numbers than CD4&#x0002b; T cells independently of precursor frequency. <source>Clin. Immunol.</source> <volume>113</volume>:<fpage>89</fpage>&#x02013;100.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15380534</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal">Smith, A.L., M.E. Wikstrom, and B. Fazekas de St Groth. <year>2000</year>. Visualizing T cell competition for peptide/MHC complexes: a specific mechanism to minimize the effect of precursor frequency. <source>Immunity.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>783</fpage>&#x02013;794.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11163194</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal">Morgan, D.J., H.T. Kreuwel, and L.A. Sherman. <year>1999</year>. Antigen concentration and precursor frequency determine the rate of CD8&#x0002b; T cell tolerance to peripherally expressed antigens. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>163</volume>:<fpage>723</fpage>&#x02013;727.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10395663</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal">Mintern, J.D., G.M. Davey, G.T. Belz, F.R. Carbone, and W.R. Heath. <year>2002</year>. Cutting edge: precursor frequency affects the helper dependence of cytotoxic T cells. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>168</volume>:<fpage>977</fpage>&#x02013;980.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11801627</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal">Suchin, E.J., P.B. Langmuir, E. Palmer, M.H. Sayegh, A.D. Wells, and L.A. Turka. <year>2001</year>. Quantifying the frequency of alloreactive T cells in vivo: new answers to an old question. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>166</volume>:<fpage>973</fpage>&#x02013;981.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11145675</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal">Blattman, J.N., R. Antia, D.J. Sourdive, X. Wang, S.M. Kaech, K. Murali-Krishna, J.D. Altman, and R. Ahmed. <year>2002</year>. Estimating the precursor frequency of naive antigen-specific CD8 T cells. <source>J. Exp. Med.</source> <volume>195</volume>:<fpage>657</fpage>&#x02013;664.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11877489</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal">Whitmire, J.K., N. Benning, and J.L. Whitton. <year>2006</year>. Precursor frequency, nonlinear proliferation, and functional maturation of virus-specific CD4&#x0002b; T cells. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>176</volume>:<fpage>3028</fpage>&#x02013;3036.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16493061</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal">Song, H.K., H. Noorchashm, Y.K. Lieu, S. Rostami, S.A. Greeley, C.F. Barker, and A. Naji. <year>1999</year>. Cutting edge: alloimmune responses against major and minor histocompatibility antigens: distinct division kinetics and requirement for CD28 costimulation. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>162</volume>:<fpage>2467</fpage>&#x02013;2471.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10072484</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal">Kishimoto, K., S. Sandner, J. Imitola, M. Sho, Y. Li, P.B. Langmuir, D.M. Rothstein, T.B. Strom, L.A. Turka, and M.H. Sayegh. <year>2002</year>. Th1 cytokines, programmed cell death, and alloreactive T cell clone size in transplant tolerance. <source>J. Clin. Invest.</source> <volume>109</volume>:<fpage>1471</fpage>&#x02013;1479.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12045261</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal">Wells, A.D., H. Gudmundsdottir, and L.A. Turka. <year>1997</year>. Fol